.TH PCBPLACE 1
.SH NAME
pcbplace \- place the parts of a board
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pcbplace
[
.B \-v
] [
.B \-g
.I group
] [
.B \-L
.I lib
] ...
.I board.pcb
[
.I lock
]
.RI > new.lock
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pcbplace
reads the board source,
.IR pcb (5),
its netlist and footprints and the lock on stdin, and writes the lock
with every part placed.
A part keeps its place if the statement that placed it is unchanged and
the parts it is placed against kept theirs; every other part is placed
anew among the others as they stand, which nothing moves.
Placement is best effort
.RI ( pcb (5)):
the same source and lock need not give the same places, and later
placers may be stochastic.
The fixed parts come first, then the rest in the order their anchors
allow.
A part without
.B rot
is turned the way, of four, that brings its pads nearest the placed
pads of their nets; then it moves out its way until its courtyard is
clear of the parts on its side, of the keep-out of every hole
.RI ( pcbfp (5))
and of every plated hole, inside the board.
A part placed of a thing stands next to it, its origin, or its pad of
the pin said by
.BR by ,
on the middle of the thing (a part, or a group placed whole first)
across the way.
A part placed near a thing
.RB ( near ,
.IR pcb (5))
takes the nearest spot where its courtyard is clear, out from the
thing, at its gap at least, the way said or any; a block placed near a
thing moves whole to the nearest spot where all its parts stand clear.
No part moves for a designator: the designators find their room among
the parts as placed.
A group laid out alone whose root is fixed keeps clear of the parts
fixed outside it, as they stand from its root.
What is not moved, a part fixed or kept from the lock or a block fixed
by its root, and comes on another part or a keep-out, is a fault: its
line and both parts.
A place statement of no form of
.IR pcb (5),
or a fix or place statement whose rot is not 0, 90, 180 or 270,
places nothing and is a fault, told once, naming the word that is
wrong (a
.B by
of a pin not of the part, or not of an
.B of
statement, too); a pin placed from that is not there is a fault, told
once.
With
.B \-v
each placement is told on stderr with its line and rule; it and every
fault name a part by its name and designator (ch1.gain.rf (R401)).
The lock comes from the file
.IR lock ,
or stdin; a file that is not there is nothing locked yet.
A run lays out the whole board by its groups: each group of a
.B block
statement alone, the deepest first (of one depth in the order of their
names), each in its section of the lock, then the board, of its blocks
(pcb(5)).
A copy
.RB ( block
.I COPY
.B as
.IR SOURCE )
is not laid out: after SOURCE, its sections become SOURCE's, renamed
part for part and net for net.
With
.B \-g
.I group
it lays out that group alone: its parts, its root at the origin, so the
lock is the group's; a group of a
.B block
statement in it (the largest such) is placed whole, rigid, from its own
lock (pcb(5)).
With
.B \-v
it also tells the difficulty of the group laid out: its parts, the
parts alike on the same nets counted once and each block one.
.SH EXAMPLES
pcbplace outputs.pcb outputs.lock | pcbroute outputs.pcb > new.lock
.SH SEE ALSO
pcb(5), pcbroute(1), pcbcheck(1), pcb-howto(7)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
FILE:LINE: and the fault, each on a line; exit status 1 on any.
